A Dockerfile is a text blueprint containing a sequence of instructions used by the Docker builder to assemble a container image. By convention, instructions are written in UPPERCASE and are followed by their arguments.
The table below covers the most common Dockerfile instructions, their primary purposes, and standard syntax structures:

Core Dockerfile Instructions Reference


Instruction	Purpose	Common Syntax Example
FROM	Sets the base image for subsequent instructions. Must be the first command.	FROM ubuntu:22.04
WORKDIR	Sets the working directory inside the container for any following commands.	WORKDIR /app
COPY	Copies files or directories from your host machine into the container filesystem.	COPY . .
ADD	Similar to COPY, but can also handle remote URLs and unpack compressed files.	ADD archive.tar.gz /tmp/
RUN	Executes commands during the build process (e.g., installing packages) and creates a new layer.	RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y curl
ENV	Sets environment variables that persist both during build and at runtime.	ENV NODE_ENV=production
ARG	Defines variables that users can pass only at build-time via --build-arg.	ARG VERSION=1.0
CMD	Specifies the default command to execute when the container starts. Can be overridden.	CMD ["node", "server.js"]
ENTRYPOINT	Configures a container to run as an executable. Arguments passed at runtime append to it.	ENTRYPOINT ["python3", "app.py"]
EXPOSE	Documents the network ports that the container listens on at runtime.	EXPOSE 8080
VOLUME	Creates a mount point for persistent or externally shared data.	VOLUME ["/data"]
USER	Sets the username or UID to use when running subsequent instructions to avoid root access.	USER node
HEALTHCHECK	Tells Docker how to test the container to check if it is still working properly.	`HEALTHCHECK CMD curl --fail http://localhost/
LABEL	Adds custom metadata (tags, maintainer info, versioning) to the image.	LABEL version="1.0"


Crucial Concepts to Keep in Mind

• Comments: Any line starting with # is treated as a comment and ignored by the builder.
• Exec vs. Shell Form: Commands like RUN, CMD, and ENTRYPOINT can be written in two ways:
	• Exec Form (Preferred): CMD ["executable", "param1"] — does not invoke a command shell, properly passing system signals like SIGTERM.
	• Shell Form: CMD executable param1 — runs the command as a substring through /bin/sh -c.
• Layering & Caching: Instructions like RUN, COPY, and ADD create read-only layers. Grouping commands together (e.g., combining multiple RUN statements with &&) keeps the final image size small and utilizes build caching efficiently.
